Origin {{unc|en|nocap=1}}; perhaps from {{cog|nl|pinken||blink}} or the English verb {{m|en|pink#Etymology 6|pink}} from the same source.<ref>{{R:OED Online|pink|v.2|144212|June 2006}}</ref> Perhaps from the notion of the petals being {{m|en|pink#Etymology 4|pinked}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{senseid|en|color}} A colour reminiscent of pinks, the flowers. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|My new dress is a wonderful shade of '''pink'''.}}
#: {{color panel|pink|F52887}}
#: {{color panel|light pink|FF80BF}}
#* {{RQ:Hollinghurst Line|page=228|passage=Jasper couldn't have known they'd been overheard upstairs, but his little smirk coming and going invited you to guess he'd been up to something. He had the '''pink''' of sex about him still.}}
## [[magenta|Magenta]], the colour evoked by red and blue light when combined.
## [[pale|Pale]] [[red]].
# Any of various [[flowers]] of that colour in the genus {{taxfmt|Dianthus|genus}}, sometimes called [[carnation]]s. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|This garden in particular has a beautiful bed of '''pinks'''.}}
# {{lb|en|dated}} A perfect example; [[excellence]], [[perfection]]; the embodiment {{m|en|of}} some quality. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|Your hat, madam, is the very '''pink''' of fashion.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Romeo and Juliet|II|iv|passage=Nay, I am the very '''pink''' of courtesy.}}
# [[hunting pink|Hunting pink]]; [[scarlet]], as worn by hunters. {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1928|author=w:Siegfried Sassoon|title=Memoirs of a Fox-Hunting Man|page=23|publisher=Penguin|year_published=2013
|passage=I had taken it for granted that there would be people ‘in '''pink'''’, but these enormous confident strangers overwhelmed me with the visible authenticity of their brick-red coats.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1986|author=Michael J. O'Shea|title=James Joyce and Heraldry|publisher=SUNY|page=69
|passage=it is interesting to note the curious legend that the '''pink''' of the hunting field is not due to any optical advantage but to an entirely different reason.}}
# {{lb|en|snooker}} One of the [[colour]] balls used in [[snooker]], coloured pink, with a value of 6 points. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|Oh dear, he's left himself snookered behind the '''pink'''.}}
# {{lb|en|slang}} An unlettered and uncultured, but relatively prosperous, member of the middle classes; compare {{m|en|Babbitt}}, {{m|en|bourgeoisie}}.
# {{alt form|en|pinko}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1981|author=Edwin R. Bayley|title=Joe McCarthy and the Press|page=163|text=My own guess is that there are some '''pinks''' in the State Department and in other government departments and agencies, and of course they should be found and ousted; but it seems to me that this can be done without besmirching innocent people and without making such broadside charges that people will lose faith in all government.|quotee=Ben Hibbs}}
# {{lb|en|slang}} The [[vagina]] or [[vulva]]. <!--is this uncountable? e.g. showing some pink in pornography?-->
#* {{quote-book|en|date=2020-03-23|author=Mike Hatch|title=The Dumb Class: Boomer Junior High|publisher=Mike Hatch H&A Publishing|isbn=9780965722520|page=78
|text=Then Eddie did what he calls, 'Two in the '''pink''', one in the stink.' “I held up my right forefinger and middle finger and said, “Two.” Then I held up my ring finger and said “One. Two in the pussy, one in the ass.”}}

